--------------------------------------------------------------------
CURRENT ASSET STATUS  -  placeholders, not the client's work
--------------------------------------------------------------------
NO IMAGE IN THIS REPOSITORY IS THE CLIENT'S WORK.

Every photograph is a stand-in fetched by tools/placeholders.py from
Lorem Picsum, which serves photography from Unsplash. They are here to
test layout, legibility, proportion and loading against real pictures,
because a grey rectangle cannot answer those questions.

They are used under the Unsplash License: free for commercial and
non-commercial use, no permission required, no attribution required.
That licence permits this use. It is not the reason they are leaving.

THEY MUST BE REMOVED BEFORE LAUNCH, and not because of any licence —
because a portfolio built from someone else's photographs
misrepresents the artist whose name is on it. A visitor cannot tell a
stand-in from a real piece, and one borrowed image quietly devalues
every real one beside it. This is why the site also carries no client
name while `live = false` in config.toml.

Every placeholder is listed in content/PLACEHOLDERS.txt, so removing
them is one command with nothing left to wonder about:

    python3 tools/placeholders.py --purge

--------------------------------------------------------------------
THE BRAND ON THIS DEMO IS A STAND-IN
--------------------------------------------------------------------
The client's brand is an established trademark. A public repository and
a public Pages URL carrying it would compete with his own referencing
for his own name, so nothing here uses it — not the rendered page, and
not the content files, which are readable on GitHub whatever the page
chooses to render.

10MILES is the stand-in, and it is deliberately not a near-miss of the
real mark: a name that half-matches is worse than one that does not
match at all. The six professional references are numbered rather than
named, and the three original formats are unnamed, for the same reason.

The real identity, organisations, roles and formats are supplied at
handover, into a checkout that is not this public one.
tools/content-check.py refuses to let them be committed here. The terms
it guards live in _ref/identifiers.txt, which is untracked — a tripwire
that names the thing it guards would be the leak it is trying to
prevent.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
THE ICONS ARE ORIGINAL, AND ALSO PLACEHOLDERS
--------------------------------------------------------------------
static/favicon.svg, static/icon-180.png and static/og.png are drawn by
tools/icons.py, here, with no dependency beyond the standard library.
Nothing is taken from an icon pack: those archives are overwhelmingly
unlicensed re-uploads, and a mark on a client's site is the last place
to discover that.

They are placeholders all the same. The graphiste authors the real logo
and iconography. The mark carries no letterform on purpose — a
letterform would be a logo, and that is hers to design.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
NO INVENTED REFERENCES
--------------------------------------------------------------------
The client's brief requires that missing content stays visibly flagged
and is never replaced by fictional references in anything public. No
company, role, date or credit may be invented to fill a gap — not even
temporarily, and not even while `live = false`.

